## 5. C# Coding Standards

### Naming Conventions
```csharp
// Classes
public class UserAuthenticationService { }

// Interfaces (prefix with I)
public interface IUserRepository { }

// Private fields (leading underscore, camelCase)
private readonly ILogger _logger;
private string _username;

// Properties (PascalCase)
public string Username { get; set; }
public int UserId { get; set; }

// Methods (PascalCase)
public async Task<User> GetUserByIdAsync(int id) { }
public void LogError(string message) { }

// Local variables (camelCase)
var userName = "John";
```

### Code Organization
```csharp
public class MyService
{
    // 1. Constants
    private const int DefaultTimeout = 30;
    
    // 2. Fields
    private readonly ILogger _logger;
    private readonly IRepository _repository;
    
    // 3. Constructor
    public MyService(ILogger logger, IRepository repository)
{
        _logger = logger;
        _repository = repository;
    }
    
    // 4. Public methods
    public async Task<T> GetDataAsync(int id) { }
    
    // 5. Private methods
    private void ValidateInput(string input) { }
}
```

### Async/Await
- Always use async/await for I/O operations
- Method names ending in `Async` for async methods
- Never use `.Result` or `.Wait()` - use `await`
- Use `CancellationToken` for long-running operations

### Exception Handling
```csharp
try
{
    var result = await _repository.GetAsync(id);
    _logger.LogInformation($"Retrieved item {id}");
    return result;
}
catch (ArgumentException ex)
{
    _logger.LogError($"Invalid argument: {ex.Message}");
    throw;
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _logger.LogError($"Unexpected error: {ex.Message}");
    throw;
}
```

